      "key": "syntheses",
      "title": "Syntheses and notes",
      "items": [
        {
          "tags": [
            "cardiology",
            "summary"
          ],
          "flags": [],
          "isAi": true,
          "body": "The cohort study followed 4,812 adults for a median of 6.4 years. Participants who reported more than 150 minutes of moderate activity each week had a lower risk of cardiovascular events than those who reported none, and the gap widened after adjustment for age, smoking and baseline blood pressure.\n\nThe authors caution that physical activity was self-reported at enrolment only. People who change their habits during follow-up are counted in their original group, which probably understates the true effect. They also note that the participants were recruited from primary care clinics in two regions, so the findings may not carry over to other populations.\n\nIn a sensitivity analysis that excluded the first two years of follow-up, the association was slightly weaker but still clear. The authors read this as evidence against reverse causation, where early illness reduces activity and also raises risk.",
          "id": "s1",
          "kind": "note",
          "title": "Activity and heart risk: what the evidence says",
          "citationNumber": 1,
          "source": {
            "label": "AI summary of 3 clips"
          },
          "capturedAt": "2026-09-28T10:00:00Z"
        },
        {
          "tags": [
            "open questions"
          ],
          "flags": [
            {
              "kind": "verify",
              "text": "Verify source: the trial protocol was amended after enrolment began."
            }
          ],
          "isAi": true,
          "body": "Three questions remain open: whether the effect holds in adults over 75, whether the dose response is linear, and whether wearable data would change the picture.\n\nA second round of analysis by Ramírez and colleagues is due next year.",
          "id": "s2",
          "kind": "note",
          "title": "Open questions from the Möller trial",
          "capturedAt": "2026-09-29T08:30:00Z"
        },
        {
          "tags": [
            "methods"
          ],
          "flags": [],
          "isAi": false,
          "body": "The sample is large enough for the main result, but the subgroup of adults over 75 has only 212 people.",
          "id": "s3",
          "kind": "note",
          "title": "My reading notes on sample size",
          "notes": "Ask Dr. Ramírez whether the subgroup was pre-specified. If not, I should treat it as exploratory.",
          "capturedAt": "2026-09-30T14:15:00Z"
        }
      ]
